<p class="lead">Most landscape lighting systems do not fail all at once. A fixture goes dark, another starts to look dim, a zone flickers after a storm, and one day the lights are on at noon. Spring Landscape Lighting provides landscape lighting repair in Spring, TX for systems of every age and brand, from a single broken path light to a tired halogen installation that needs a full LED upgrade. We find the actual cause, fix it properly, and tell you plainly whether a repair or a redesign makes more sense.</p>

<h2>What we repair and upgrade</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Dim, dead or flickering fixtures, broken lenses and corroded sockets.</li>
  <li>Cut, nicked or exposed wire from edging, aeration, trenching or new landscaping.</li>
  <li>Failed, undersized or overloaded transformers, tripped GFCIs and bad timers or photocells.</li>
  <li>Voltage drop problems on long runs or overloaded circuits.</li>
  <li>Fixtures buried by mulch, swallowed by shrubs or knocked out of aim.</li>
  <li>Halogen-to-LED conversions and partial or complete redesigns of older systems.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common symptoms and likely causes</h2>
<p>Here is a quick diagnostic guide. Always turn off the transformer before handling fixtures or wire, and call an electrician for any problem on the 120-volt side.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>What we check</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Fixtures dim, especially at the end of a run</td><td>Voltage drop from long runs, undersized wire or too much load on one circuit</td><td>Voltage at the fixtures, wire gauge, run length, transformer tap</td></tr>
<tr><td>Whole zone or whole system out</td><td>Tripped GFCI outlet, transformer failure, blown breaker or fuse, timer or photocell failure, or a cut main wire</td><td>Power at the outlet, transformer output, controls, main run</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lights flicker or cut in and out</td><td>Loose or corroded connection, damaged wire, or incompatible LED and transformer pairing</td><td>Splices, connectors, wire integrity, lamp compatibility</td></tr>
<tr><td>One fixture dead, others fine</td><td>Burned-out lamp, corroded socket, water inside the fixture or a damaged lead wire</td><td>Lamp, socket, fixture seal, connection to the main line</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lights on during the day</td><td>Faulty, dirty or shaded photocell, or a timer stuck in the on position</td><td>Photocell location and condition, timer settings, override switch</td></tr>
<tr><td>Some fixtures much brighter than others</td><td>Mixed lamp types or wattages, uneven voltage, or LED retrofits mixed with halogen</td><td>Lamp inventory, voltage at each fixture</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="callout callout--warn"><strong>Safety:</strong> The low-voltage side of a landscape system is generally safe to inspect, but the transformer plugs into or is wired to a 120-volt circuit. If you suspect a problem with the outlet, breaker or house wiring, call a licensed electrician.</div>

<h2>How we troubleshoot</h2>
<p>Good repair is methodical. We start at the power source and work outward: outlet and GFCI, transformer output on each tap, timer and photocell, then voltage at fixtures along each run. Most LED fixtures are designed to operate within a range of roughly 9 to 15 volts, while halogen lamps are much fussier and dim noticeably below about 11 volts. Measuring voltage tells us whether the problem is the fixture, the wire or the supply. Our article on <a href="/landscape-lighting-transformer-sizing/">sizing a landscape lighting transformer</a> explains the 80% load rule and voltage drop in more detail.</p>

<h2>Why systems fail in Spring, TX</h2>
<div class="grid-cards">
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Water and humidity</h3><p>Heavy rain, irrigation and humid air get into poorly sealed connections. Twist-on caps and electrical tape corrode within a few seasons.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Landscaping work</h3><p>Edging, aeration, new beds and fence repairs are the leading cause of cut wires we find.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Growth and storms</h3><p>Shrubs swallow fixtures, roots lift wire, and storms knock fixtures out of aim or bury them in debris.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Heat on equipment</h3><p>Transformers and timers on sun-baked walls run hot, and older units often fail first in late summer.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Halogen to LED upgrades</h2>
<p>Many older systems around Spring still run halogen lamps at 20 to 50 watts each. Switching to LED fixtures or lamps in the 2 to 12 watt range typically cuts lighting energy by roughly 75 to 85 percent, reduces lamp replacements dramatically and lightens the load on the transformer and wire. It is not always as simple as swapping bulbs: some older transformers and fixtures do not pair well with LED retrofits, and mixed systems can look uneven. Use the calculator below to estimate your savings, and read our full guide to <a href="/halogen-to-led-landscape-lighting/">converting halogen landscape lighting to LED</a>.</p>

<?php tool_embed('led-savings-calculator'); ?>

<h2>Common mistakes we avoid</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Replacing lamps without finding the cause.</strong> A new lamp in a corroded socket fails again. We fix connections, not just symptoms.</li>
  <li><strong>Taping splices.</strong> Every repair connection we make is sealed with waterproof, direct-burial-rated connectors.</li>
  <li><strong>Adding fixtures to an overloaded transformer.</strong> We check total load against the 80% guideline before expanding anything.</li>
  <li><strong>Mixing color temperatures.</strong> When we replace fixtures or lamps, we match the color temperature so the system still looks like one design.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Repair, upgrade or redesign?</h2>
<p>Sometimes a few targeted repairs restore the whole system. Other times the original layout was the real problem: lights in the wrong places, glare, or a design the landscape has outgrown. In those cases, it may be worth reworking the plan, adding <a href="/services/smart-lighting-controls/">smart lighting controls</a>, rebalancing <a href="/services/architectural-lighting/">architectural lighting</a>, or relighting trees with our <a href="/services/tree-garden-lighting/">tree and garden lighting</a> service. Spring Landscape Lighting will give you an honest recommendation either way. For routine upkeep between visits, see our <a href="/landscape-lighting-maintenance-checklist/">landscape lighting maintenance checklist</a>.</p>

